hi Miguel,

please allow me to take issue with you one just one thing: "p is evidently one", you say. This is not so obvious. There are several studies that have formally estimated p in plants and it turned out that neither at the level of the individual plant nor at that of the patch was it equal to one. See for instance Kery et al. (Journal of Ecology, 2006) and Kery and Gregg (JEcol, ca. 2003). Furthermore, I am conducing a large-scale anaysis right now of p in a random sample of 100 species among the ~1700 vascular plants in Switzerland: on average, p of species detection along 2.5 km transects is only about 0.8.

Of course, the good thing with having temporal reps and a great software such as colext() allows one to test formally  the assumption p=1.

Kind regards  ---  Marc

