A comment on the p=1 situation:
(1) if you don't have any replicates, maybe this question is immaterial -- you might have to assume p=1.
(2) to do that, you could replicate your data set (i.e., create a 2nd replicate). This would look exactly like the 1st replicate and so it should force p=1 (maybe).
(3) if you do have replicates then either (a) sometimes you have a y=0 where there was previously or subsequently a y=1, in which case p definitely is not 1. (b) if you always have complete consistency in that regard, then it does suggest p=1 and the posterior mass should pile-up on p=1.

andy

